Title: RACOON RESCUE - V2
Credit: Scene Draft
Author: Scene Writer Agent
Draft date: January 2026

===

/* THE SURRENDER - Danny stops fighting */
/* Approximate page count: 1-1.5 pages */
/* Power arc: CHAOS -> STILLNESS */
/* This is the emotional resolution. The absence of "It's fine." */

EXT. SUBURBAN STREET - CRASH SITE - NIGHT

Blue and red lights pulse across the wreckage. The colors wash
over everything in slow, hypnotic waves. Police. Ambulance.
Fire truck. The full machinery of consequence.

Danny's truck sits nose-first into a hedge. Steam rises from
the crumpled hood. The driver's door hangs open.

GERALD's minivan is stopped at an angle twenty feet away. The
"HONOR STUDENT" bumper sticker now reads "HONOR STU" where
the collision tore it.

CHAOS everywhere:

- PARTY-GOERS from a nearby house spill onto lawns, phones
  raised, recording everything
- FIREFIGHTERS spray foam on something hissing under Danny's hood
- A PARAMEDIC tends to MARGARET in the back of an ambulance,
  a blanket around her shoulders
- GERALD stands with TWO COPS, gesturing wildly, pointing at
  Danny, at the truck, at the sky

And in the middle of it all:

DANNY.

He sits on the curb. Hands in his lap. Completely still.

The world spins around him in blue and red strobe, but Danny
has stopped moving. For the first time all night, for the
first time in months, maybe for the first time in years--

He is not trying to fix anything.

CLOSE ON: Danny's hands.

His fingers rest on his knees. No movement. No welder's tic.
No thumb rubbing against forefinger, checking for burns that
are not there.

Just stillness.

ANGLE ON: Danny's face.

Blood runs from a cut above his eyebrow. Scratches rake down
his cheek--raccoon's last gift. His eyes are wet. Not crying,
exactly. Leaking. The overflow of a container finally allowed
to be full.

HIGH ANGLE - looking down on Danny.

He is SMALL in frame. A man surrounded by the machinery of
his own destruction. Fire trucks and police cars and gawking
strangers with their phones. The chaos he created, spread out
around him like evidence.

He does not look up.

A UNIFORMED COP approaches. Young. Clipboard in hand. He
crouches to Danny's level.

COP
Sir? Sir, are you injured?

Danny doesn't answer immediately. He looks at his hands.
At the blood on his fingers. At the lights painting everything
red, then blue, then red again.

COP (CONT'D)
Sir? Can you hear me?

DANNY
(quiet)
Yeah.

COP
Are you injured?

A beat. Danny's jaw works. This is the moment. The old Danny
would say: I'm fine. The old Danny would deflect, minimize,
insist he had it under control.

DANNY
(barely above a whisper)
Yeah. Yeah, I think so.

The cop nods. Writes something on his clipboard.

COP
Okay. We're going to get you looked
at. Just stay right here.

The cop moves away. Danny stays.

In the background, GERALD's voice rises, indistinct. Angry.
Margaret says something to calm him. It doesn't work.

Danny doesn't react. He watches the lights pulse.

INSERT - DANNY'S LAP:

His PHONE lies on the asphalt beside him. Cracked screen.
Spider-web fractures across the display.

But visible through the damage, glowing faintly:

RECENT CALLS
SPONSOR MIKE - DO NOT ANSWER AFTER 2100

Danny looks at it. Looks away.

Looks back.

He reaches down. Picks up the phone. Holds it like something
fragile. Something that might still work.

CLOSE ON: Danny's face.

He doesn't dial. Not yet. But he holds the phone. And for the
first time, he doesn't put it away.

He doesn't say: It's fine.

He doesn't say: This is fine.

He doesn't say anything at all.

He just sits there. Present. Still. A man who has finally
stopped running from the thing that was always going to
catch him.

The blue and red lights wash over his face.

His eyes close.

MATCH CUT TO:

INT. NAVY JAG COURTROOM - DAY

Danny's eyes open.

Same face. Cleaned up now. The scratches have healed to faint
lines. The bandage on his ear is fresh. The dress whites are
immaculate.

But the stillness remains.

COMMANDER WELLS (O.S.)
Petty Officer Vega?

Danny blinks. Returns to the present. To the witness stand.
To the weight of everything that happened.

COMMANDER WELLS
Are you ready to continue?

Danny looks at the paw print in the evidence bag. At Gerald
in the gallery. At his own hands, folded in his lap.

Still.

DANNY
Yes, ma'am.

He takes a breath.

DANNY (CONT'D)
I am now.

FADE TO:

/* End scene - approximately 1.5 pages */
/* Note: The absence of "It's fine" is the character beat. */
/* The phone in his hand is the visual promise of change. */
/* The match cut returns us to the frame - completing the loop. */
